          ],
          "text": "This 72-year-old male was admitted as an emergency with a 3-day history of colicky abdominal pain, distension, vomiting, and absolute constipation, with a primary diagnosis of adhesional small bowel obstruction.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.12",
          "part": "part_b",
          "heading": "admission_summary",
          "fact_ids": [
            "key_investigations.0",
            "treatment.0",
            "treatment.1",
            "treatment.2",
            "treatment.3",
            "treatment.4",
            "treatment.5",
            "treatment.6",
            "treatment.7"
          ],
          "text": "CT abdomen confirmed adhesional small bowel obstruction with a transition point identified and no ischaemia on initial imaging. He was initially managed conservatively with nasogastric tube decompression and IV fluids. Warfarin was held and his INR reversed in anticipation of surgery. He subsequently underwent emergency laparotomy with adhesiolysis, resection of a non-viable small bowel segment, and primary anastomosis; a defunctioning loop ileostomy was formed. He recovered post-operatively in HDU. Stoma nurse teaching was commenced prior to discharge. Metformin, ramipril, and bisoprolol were restarted on day 6, and warfarin was restarted with bridging treatment-dose enoxaparin until his INR is therapeutic.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
