    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came in with 3 days of cramping tummy pain, a swollen tummy, vomiting, and not being able to open your bowels.\n\nThe team found that your small bowel (part of your gut) was blocked. This was caused by adhesions (bands of scar tissue), most likely from your previous appendix operation."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "A CT scan of your tummy confirmed the blockage. It showed where the blockage was and that the bowel was not damaged at that point.\n\nThe team first tried to treat the blockage without an operation. This involved a tube passed through your nose into your stomach, and a drip to give you fluids. This is sometimes called \"drip and suck\".\n\nA blood-thinning medicine (warfarin) was stopped and its effects were reversed in case you needed surgery.\n\nYou then had an emergency operation. The surgeon:\n- removed the scar tissue causing the blockage\n- removed a small part of the bowel that was not healthy\n- joined the bowel back together\n- formed a stoma (an opening on your tummy) called a loop ileostomy\n\nAfter the operation you recovered in the high dependency unit (HDU).\n\nA stoma nurse started teaching you how to care for your stoma.\n\nOn day 6, a blood pressure medicine (ramipril), a heart rate medicine (bisoprolol), and a diabetes medicine (metformin) were restarted.\n\nWarfarin was also restarted. You were given treatment-dose injections (enoxaparin) alongside it until the warfarin reached the right level."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "- A community stoma nurse will follow you up at home.\n- You have a surgical outpatient clinic appointment at 6 weeks. At a later stage, reversing the ileostomy may be considered.\n- Your GP will check your warfarin level (INR)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{SAFETY_NET}}"
        }
      ],
